Watches and protects the hosts behind the firewall (e.g. customer
servers): detects attacks on them (reflection, floods, brute force,
scans; with Suricata and CrowdSec), puts the sources of chosen attack
types into virusprot through block conditions, and shows pf state and
rule limits, blocked traffic and which ports and services each host
uses as graphs and tables under Reporting: HostGuard. Identifies the
services hosts serve by testing the ports (banner, HTTP, TLS, Redis,
PostgreSQL, RDP, DNS, NTP, optionally nmap -sV).

Plugin Changelog
================

1.7

* Renamed from os-lanusage to os-hostguard; the package and install.sh stop
  the old plugin and move its database, a model migration takes over its
  settings; an os-lanusage package is removed under System: Firmware: Plugins
  (install.sh does it)
* Privileges split: Reporting (dashboard), Services: Settings (settings,
  service, clearing data), Firewall: Block conditions
* Attacks tab: attack episodes per LAN host (start, end, peak, sources, share
  that passed the firewall), classified as reflection / amplification, SYN /
  UDP / ICMP flood, distributed brute force or port scan; from the existing
  samples, no extra capture
* Suricata alerts towards LAN hosts (eve.json, read incrementally with a cap),
  per host and signature; severity 1 alerts raise an alert
* CrowdSec: the scenario of each banned outside source (cscli, every 5 minutes)
* Block conditions: "block from now on" on an attack, a Suricata alert or an
  outside source at a rule limit; matching outside sources go into virusprot
  (never LAN hosts, the firewall or trusted networks), released when their
  time is up or the condition is removed; own tab, removable any time; own
  privilege "Firewall: HostGuard: Block conditions"; the table is synced again
  every 10 minutes (reboot, flush), at most 50000 addresses. An attack
  condition takes the sources of the attack only (not every visitor of the
  host); with global source tracking a rule limit condition takes only
  sources that have a state of that rule
* Outside addresses are grouped on the Blocked and Limits tabs, every one of
  them is listed on the Attacks tab
* Owner (AS, country) and network type of every outside source: cloud, CDN,
  VPN, hosting, online service, else the AS category (ISP, business,
  education, government; marked as an estimate), and its role (Tor exit,
  crawler, monitoring, iCloud Private Relay); a summary by addresses and by
  packets and the busiest owners. Free lists that need no account, downloaded
  once a day (a missed list once more three hours later) into a local
  database, the more reliable one winning where they
  disagree: official provider ranges and the Tor exit list, the official lists
  rezmoss/cloud-provider-ip-addresses collects, AS derived lists (X4BNet
  lists_vpn), ipverse/as-metadata categories, iptoasn.com, Spamhaus ASN-DROP
* Automatic filtering in three levels by how sure the detection is: 1 port
  scans / sweeps seen in blocked attempts and Spamhaus ASN-DROP networks,
  2 brute force repeaters (not on mail / web login ports) and Suricata
  severity 1, 3 flood repeaters pf also blocked, Suricata severity 2 and per
  source limits; off by default; crawlers, monitoring, Private Relay, CDNs and
  reflection sources are never taken
* Blocked going out: an attempt that passed in (e.g. pass all on the LAN) but
  has no state on the outgoing interface is reported as blocked there
* Direction of every per source limit: "→ in (clients)" when outside addresses
  open the connections, "← out (your hosts)" when your LAN hosts do; taken from
  the rule (from / to, interface, direction) and shown on rules, sources and alerts
* Global source tracking: a LAN host is no longer compared with the limits of
  inbound rules and an outside client with those of outbound rules
* States / Limits hosts table separates the three things: share of the pf state
  table, the strictest per source rule limit of the host, and the HostGuard
  warning threshold (an alert setting, not a pf limit)
* Blocked traffic sampling captures packets where they enter (tcpdump -Q in):
  outgoing packets on the upstream bridge member no longer look blocked
* Blocked traffic: past the 50 busiest ports of a host and direction per poll the
  rest is stored as "other ports" (a flood on random ports no longer adds a row
  per port)
* The first poll after a (re)start takes the existing connections as its
  baseline instead of counting them as new with all their bytes
* An emptied "Watch pf tables" field now watches no tables (it used to fall back
  to virusprot, sshlockout, crowdsec)
* max-src-conn-rate shown with its time window; estimated values marked "est.";
  clicking a host keeps the current tab; "Acknowledge all open" asks first;
  the direction filter on the Blocked tab no longer empties the "Why" columns

1.6

* Rule limits read from every rule: max, max-src-nodes, max-src-states,
  max-src-conn, max-src-conn-rate, with their fill ratio and busiest source
* Global source tracking (pf's default, what OPNsense rules use) is now
  matched; 1.4/1.5 only matched "source-track rule" nodes
* Traffic shaper: pipes / queues with their bandwidth limit, mask, description
  and shaper rules, throughput and drops per IP, "Shaper limit reached" alerts
* Block reasons: shaper drops, sources near a limit, rule max states full

1.5

* Why blocked, without logging: each sampled blocked attempt is matched against
  the tables that block rules use (CrowdSec, virusprot, bogons, aliases), per
  source limits, a full state table and bogon sources; otherwise "no pass rule"
* "Why blocked" chart, reason per host and per attempt, reasons in the alerts
* Interfaces are shown with their OPNsense name, e.g. igb0 (WAN)

1.4

* Per source limits (max-src-states, max-src-conn, max-src-conn-rate) are read
  from the pf source tracking table: alerts name the source address and the
  rule at the limit instead of guessing from the busiest hosts
* "Sources at a per source rule limit" table on the States / Limits tab

1.3

* Blocked traffic without firewall logging: bounded sampling of connection
  attempts checked against the state table, exact block rule counters
* Alerts for LAN hosts sending blocked traffic, attacks blocked towards LAN
  hosts and busy block rules; "Blocked traffic" dashboard tab
* Reading the firewall log is now optional and off by default

1.2

* State / limit checks: pf state table usage, limit counters (memory, state-limit,
